{"subjectId":"sql","id":"classic-puzzles","title":"More Classic SQL Puzzles","group":"Interview Prep","order":62,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">A grab-bag of questions that test whether you think in sets: relational division, conditional counts, comparisons against aggregates, and self-referencing updates.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-divide\"></i>Customers who bought every product in a category (relational division)</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>-- customers who bought ALL Furniture products (Desk and Chair)\nSELECT c.name\nFROM customers c\nJOIN orders o       ON o.customer_id = c.customer_id\nJOIN order_items oi ON oi.order_id   = o.order_id\nJOIN products p     ON p.product_id  = oi.product_id\nWHERE p.category = 'Furniture'\nGROUP BY c.customer_id, c.name\nHAVING COUNT(DISTINCT p.product_id) = (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM products WHERE category = 'Furniture')\nORDER BY c.name;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>name\n-----\nSneha</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-calendar-check\"></i>Customers who ordered in every month of Q1</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT c.name\nFROM customers c JOIN orders o ON o.customer_id = c.customer_id\nWHERE o.order_date &gt;= '2024-01-01' AND o.order_date &lt; '2024-04-01'\nGROUP BY c.customer_id, c.name\nHAVING COUNT(DISTINCT strftime('%m', o.order_date)) = 3;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>name\n------\nAnanya</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-link-off\"></i>Products never ordered together with a Laptop</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT p.name FROM products p\nWHERE p.name &lt;&gt; 'Laptop'\n  AND NOT EXISTS (\n      SELECT 1\n      FROM order_items a\n      JOIN order_items b ON b.order_id = a.order_id\n      JOIN products l    ON l.product_id = b.product_id AND l.name = 'Laptop'\n      WHERE a.product_id = p.product_id\n  )\nORDER BY p.name;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>name\n--------\nChair\nDesk\nMonitor\nNotebook\nPen</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-percentage\"></i>Share of total per row</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT category,\n       SUM(price) AS catalogue_value,\n       ROUND(100.0 * SUM(price) / (SELECT SUM(price) FROM products), 1) AS pct\nFROM products\nGROUP BY category\nORDER BY pct DESC;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>category    | catalogue_value | pct\n------------+-----------------+-----\nElectronics | 69500           | 84.7\nFurniture   | 12500           | 15.2\nStationery  | 80              | 0.1</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-switch-horizontal\"></i>Swap two values in one UPDATE</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>CREATE TABLE people (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, name TEXT, gender TEXT);\nINSERT INTO people VALUES (1, 'A', 'm'), (2, 'B', 'f'), (3, 'C', 'm');\n\nUPDATE people SET gender = CASE gender WHEN 'm' THEN 'f' ELSE 'm' END;\nSELECT * FROM people ORDER BY id;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>id | name | gender\n---+------+-------\n1  | A    | f\n2  | B    | m\n3  | C    | f</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-left-right\"></i>Year-over-year style comparison with a self join</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>WITH monthly AS (\n    SELECT CAST(strftime('%m', o.order_date) AS INTEGER) AS m, COUNT(*) AS orders\n    FROM orders o GROUP BY m\n)\nSELECT cur.m AS month, cur.orders, prev.orders AS prev_month_orders\nFROM monthly cur\nLEFT JOIN monthly prev ON prev.m = cur.m - 1\nORDER BY cur.m;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>month | orders | prev_month_orders\n------+--------+------------------\n1     | 2      | NULL\n2     | 3      | 2\n3     | 3      | 3</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-history\"></i>Second most recent order of each customer</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT customer_id, order_id, order_date\nFROM (\n    SELECT o.*, ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY customer_id ORDER BY order_date DESC) AS rn\n    FROM orders o\n)\nWHERE rn = 2\nORDER BY customer_id;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>customer_id | order_id | order_date\n------------+----------+-----------\n1           | 103      | 2024-02-03\n2           | 102      | 2024-01-12\n3           | 104      | 2024-02-14</code></pre></figure>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How do you find customers who bought all products (relational division)?</summary><div><p>Group the customer's distinct purchased products and compare the count with the total number of products: <code>GROUP BY customer HAVING COUNT(DISTINCT product_id) = (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM products)</code>. The double-<code>NOT EXISTS</code> formulation (“there is no product the customer has not bought”) is the textbook alternative.</p></div></details></section>"}